On Saturday, August 5, 2017 at 3:26:38 PM UTC-4, Bob Adamson wrote:
>
> In an email CJL wrote:
> "Part of why I am interested [as are others] in getting all the components 
> of OS/8 accurate and up to date is so eventually when they are "lifted" we 
> don't have to unnecessarily fix them."
>
> Following the initial 3D release DEC released quite a number of patches 
> via the software newsletter for the 8. I maintained my company's test 
> department pdp8s at the time and scrupulously updated them and I think I 
> have a couple of RK05 platters from around 1980 which had all the fixes 
> (though some were optional) installed.
>
> More recently I built a system from the original (?) tapes (Van Mark's 
> site) and installed the various patches from the software news from 
> Bitsavers. I found that from time to time my images didn't have the 
> expected content of various words though I updated them anyway. It's quite 
> possible that I made the occasional mistake of course as patching with ODT 
> can be error prone. Nothing bad seemed to happen though it's difficult to 
> check some of the results. So it seems to me that it's not so easy to 
> define exactly what an "up to date" version of OS/8 (3D) actually is.
>

This is also a foolhardly situation in OS/8 that has other problems besides 
the fact you have to CAREFULLY use ODT to effect a patch:

In P?S/8, memory is NEVER saved on an exit to 07600.  And there is only the 
one exit point, etc.

The way around this is there is the GET command which asks for memory to be 
saved FIRST and then does the read-only normal startup.  Think of just how 
many times is the constant saving of memory because you *MIGHT* want to use 
it with ODT, etc. when in fact overwhemingly typically it is NOT going to 
be productive.  [Not to mention the loss of safety of write-protecting the 
device where applicable!]

In P?S/8, there is NO memory segmentation.  It's a 4K system and there is a 
virtual image of all of field 0.  Extended memory is unchanged, however 
there are ways to save it if you want.

In any case, to update a P?S/8 [basic, not the SHELL] system program you 
have to perform an absolute block reference and use BLKODT which is 
essentially ODT with all of the breakpoint functions removed, and extended 
to support logical unit number and block and the relative address in the 
range 0000-0177.  It even supports all of the superset features of P?S/8 
ODT such as formatted memory dumps [in several "flavors" such as six-bit 
ASCII text, ASCII text and 3-for-2 packed ASCII text.]

Thus, you do have to figure out where relatively speaking the block is to 
have to patch, but that;s all that need be done.

The SHELL concept is that each system program of course has a map of the 
core-image within because like OS/8, the SHELL has a unified directory.  
But in P?S/8 SHELL, the debugger will essentially be DDT as an extension of 
ODT, thus, we will reinvent the TOPS-10 program FILEDDT.  P?S/8 core-images 
already support the concept of overlays.  When P?S/8 PAL's primary segment 
loads into field 0, the symbol table is in the first overlay.  It's read in 
linearly in the input buffer later used to read source file sections, and 
then the symbols are processed by hashing them into the 
dynamically-allocated memory space which is up to an additional 16K of 
memory or less, etc.  That way, even the very first symbol is optimally 
hashed in place, etc.  After the binary/listing pass [there are 
command-line switches to enable/disable both separately] another overlay 
comes in to continue the assembly, etc.  Each system program knows where it 
was loaded from, thus the overlays are predictable offsets and logical 
units obtained from the system device handler arguments that loaded the 
primary segment, etc.

Thus, in the SHELL execution model, the same concept can be applied with 
regards to overlays.  The map will allow FILEDDT the ability to locate 
where a patch goes, etc.

A few related topics:

There is only the one universal exit point at 07600.  The image program can 
specify if it wants the exit to save memory!  The way the P?S/8 system 
handler works, there is a requirement that if you change 07600 [to a JMP, 
etc.] it stil has to work as a handler.  That is trivially achieved].  
Thus, IF directed to do so by the image program, it will cause a JMP to 
7757 where there will be a call to the handler to save all of field 0, put 
the original instruction back in 07600 and then JMP back, etc.

In general, the only programs that would want this would be images under 
debugging, clearly.  However, it is a separate concept.  thus, having only 
one universal exit address 07600 does everything OS/8 does without the 
unnecessary savings.

I already worked out all of the exit strategy code.  As a limitation, if 
there is only 4K, the data field cannot be saved on a breakpoint [but it 
will be set to CDF 00 within DDT.  Just means you cannot screw around with 
the likes of playing with the DF pointlessly on a 4K PDP-12 [which does 
have the extended memory controller] and also put a breakpoint on it.  If 
you have 8K or more, no problems whatsoever.

I love OS/8!  What a great inspiration to find what to NOT do wrong!

cjl


 

> Bob
